<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Atitlán</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Atitlán is a deep crater lake in the highlands of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, surrounded by volcanoes. From about the 13th century its southern shore was the heartland of the Tz'utujil Maya kingdom, whose capital stood at Chuitinamit near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> Atitlán. The Spanish under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>, with Kaqchikel allies, conquered the Tz'utujil there in 1524.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-atitlan.jpg" alt="Lake Atitlán" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lago_de_Atitlan_seen_from_orbit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Atitlán lies in the Sololá Department in the Guatemalan Highlands, about 50 km west-northwest of Antigua. Its name means between the waters and comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. It is about 18 km by 8 km, with a surface area of 130.1 square kilometres and a maximum depth of about 340 metres, which makes it the deepest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. It is endorheic, fed by two rivers and not draining to the sea. The basin fills a caldera formed by a supervolcanic eruption about 79,500 years ago, known as Los Chocoyos, which spread ash over some 6,000,000 square kilometres. Three volcanoes, Atitlán, San Pedro and Tolimán, have since grown on the southern side. Atitlán has erupted most recently, in 1853.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Maya communities have lived around the lake for a long time. Samabaj, a site about 55 feet below the present surface, appears to date from at least the pre-Classic period and has the remains of several groups of buildings, among them large structures thought to have been a city centre. During the Spanish conquest the Kaqchikel at first allied with the invaders against their historic enemies, the Tz'utujil and K'iche', but were themselves conquered when they refused to pay tribute.</p><p>The lake became a national park in 1955. In 1958 the black bass was introduced, at the suggestion of Pan American World Airways, to attract anglers. It eliminated more than two thirds of the native fish and contributed to the extinction of the Atitlán grebe. In the Guatemalan Civil War, from 1960 to 1996, the lake was the scene of human rights abuses as the government pursued a scorched earth policy against indigenous people assumed to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. The missionary Stanley Rother was killed in the church at Santiago Atitlán in 1981, and in 1990 soldiers fired on a protest march, killing 11 unarmed civilians. Hurricane Stan in October 2005 buried the village of Panabaj in a landslide.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The communities around the lake are rooted in living Maya heritage, kept chiefly by the Tz'utujil and Kaqchikel, with traditional dress and textiles. Santiago Atitlán, the largest, is noted for the worship of Maximón, an image that fuses Maya deities, Catholic saints and conquistador legends and is moved between the houses of a religious brotherhood each year. Panajachel is dominated by visitors. No road circles the lake, and some villages can be reached only by boat. The area grows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, avocados, corn and onions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> called it the most beautiful lake in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Atitlán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1250 they established a kingdom around <b>Lake Atitlán</b>, with their capital at Chuitinamit, on a hill near modern Santiago Atitlán.</blockquote><a href="/state/tzutujil-kingdom">Tz'utujil kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
